{"id": "2026-11-07-news-us-strikes-qeshm-again-as-tanker-crewman-dies", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "US strikes Qeshm again as tanker crewman dies", "summary": "US forces carried out what Trump called minor strikes on Iran's Qeshm Island on 6 November while a missile attack on the tanker Velos Sapphire killed a seafarer.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/7/will-trump-strike-nuclear-sites-again-as-iran-engages-cognitive", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["ongoing"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "US forces struck Iran's Qeshm Island in the Strait of Hormuz again on Friday 6 November, and on the same day a missile attack on a Greek-managed oil tanker in the Gulf killed one of its crew. Donald Trump called the US action \"minor strikes\" on Iranian air defences, told reporters the conflict is \"not a war\", and also said \"we've made a lot of progress with Iran\". That description sits awkwardly with the facts: these are repeated US attacks on Iranian territory, after strikes on the same island earlier in the week, and only a day after Trump said the US may have to \"destroy\" the rest of Iran. Iran's official news agency IRNA (Islamic Republic News Agency) reported two missiles hitting the Suza area of the island, and Iranian state TV said air defences shot down a US MQ-9 drone (a large armed surveillance drone), which the US has not confirmed. The tanker MT Velos Sapphire, flagged in the Marshall Islands, was heading to Bahrain when an explosion believed to come from a missile set it on fire; its operator, Velos Tankers, said a seafarer was found fatally injured. According to the UK Maritime Trade Operations centre (UKMTO, the Royal Navy's shipping-incident desk), October had more than 35 incidents in the Strait of Hormuz, the most of any month since the war began, and at least five ships have been attacked so far in November. Also on Friday, the State Department's Rewards for Justice programme offered up to $20 million for information on Iran's nuclear programme. On Saturday 7 November, Qatar's foreign ministry spokesperson Majed al-Ansari rejected claims on X that US–Iran talks had failed.", "why": "Washington is mixing new strikes with talk of a deal, while attacks on shipping are rising and Iran still sets conditions, including lifting sanctions and ending the US naval blockade of its ports, for reopening Hormuz.", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-us-strikes-qeshm-again-as-tanker-crewman-dies", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["United States", "Iran", "Qeshm Island", "Strait of Hormuz", "Velos Sapphire"]}
{"id": "2026-11-07-news-russian-missiles-hit-kyiv-s-northern-bridge", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russian missiles hit Kyiv's Northern Bridge", "summary": "Russia fired 36 ballistic missiles and 172 drones at Ukraine overnight into 7 November, hitting Kyiv's Northern Bridge and substations linking nuclear plants to the capital.", "url": "https://www.ukrinform.net/rubric-ato/4182380-zelensky-in-addition-to-kyiv-12-regions-of-ukraine-come-under-attack-overnight.html", "source_domain": "ukrinform.net", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["ongoing"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Russia fired 36 ballistic missiles and 172 drones at Ukraine overnight into Saturday 7 November, President Volodymyr Zelensky said; every missile was aimed at Kyiv and its region, and several hit the capital's Northern Bridge. Zelensky said Kyiv now has serious problems with electricity, water and heating. He said substations linking the Rivne and Khmelnytskyi nuclear power plants to Kyiv and central Ukraine were hit, along with substations in five other regions, and that 12 regions besides Kyiv were attacked. He said nine people were injured, including a child, and a kindergarten was partly destroyed. Ukraine's air force said that by 09:00 it had shot down or jammed only four of the missiles, against 149 of the drones, and recorded hits at 24 locations. Zelensky said Russia was \"preparing for trilateral talks by launching ballistic missiles\"; the three-way US–Russia–Ukraine talks are due in Abu Dhabi on Tuesday 10 and Wednesday 11 November.", "why": "very few of the ballistic missiles were stopped, and the attack hit the links between Ukraine's nuclear plants and its capital just as winter starts and days before talks where a ceasefire on energy targets is on Kyiv's list.", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-russian-missiles-hit-kyiv-s-northern-bridge", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-07", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Russia", "Ukraine", "Kyiv", "Volodymyr Zelensky"]}
{"id": "2026-11-07-news-trump-makes-stephen-miller-chief-of-staff", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "Trump makes Stephen Miller chief of staff", "summary": "Trump named deputy chief of staff Stephen Miller as White House chief of staff on 6 November, replacing Susie Wiles after the midterm defeat.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/stephen-miller-named-by-trump-as-new-white-house-chief-of-staff/a-79881571", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Donald Trump named his deputy chief of staff, Stephen Miller, as White House chief of staff on Friday 6 November, replacing Susie Wiles, who had held the job since January 2025. The change comes three days after Republicans lost both chambers of Congress in the Tuesday 3 November midterms. \"I wanted to make a change,\" Trump told reporters, saying Wiles had done great work and would move to his political operation. Miller, 41, has worked for Trump since the 2016 campaign; DW describes him as a key architect of the administration's hard-line immigration policy, including expanding the role of Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE, the federal deportation agency). SRF notes that in January, after US citizen Alex Pretti was shot dead in Minneapolis during a federal immigration operation, Miller called him a \"domestic terrorist\", and the backlash forced Trump to scale back the raids.", "why": "after voters rejected his party, Trump has put the official most closely tied to mass deportations in charge of the White House.", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-trump-makes-stephen-miller-chief-of-staff", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Donald Trump", "Stephen Miller", "Susie Wiles", "White House"]}
{"id": "2026-11-07-news-new-zealand-election-ends-in-near-tie", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "New Zealand election ends in near tie", "summary": "New Zealand's National and Labour parties each had just under 30% after the 7 November election, with the governing coalition one seat ahead on early results.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/7/governing-party-leads-in-new-zealands-knife-edge-vote-early-results", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "New Zealand's general election on Saturday 7 November was too close to call: with more than 80% of results counted, the governing centre-right National Party and the opposition Labour Party each had just under 30% of the party vote. Early Electoral Commission figures, as reported by Al Jazeera, gave Prime Minister Christopher Luxon's three-party coalition a majority of one seat. The centrist Opportunity Party looked set to pass the 5% threshold for entering parliament and could decide who governs. New Zealand uses mixed-member proportional representation (MMP: each voter casts one vote for a party and one for a local candidate, and seats follow the party vote). Labour leader Chris Hipkins said the next parliament \"is going to require all of us to compromise\". The final result, which includes votes cast abroad, is expected by Friday 27 November, and there is no deadline for forming a government. The campaign was fought over grocery prices and the cost of living.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-new-zealand-election-ends-in-near-tie", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-07", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["New Zealand", "Christopher Luxon", "Chris Hipkins", "Opportunity Party"]}
{"id": "2026-11-07-news-anc-slumps-to-its-worst-ever-result", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "ANC slumps to its worst ever result", "summary": "South Africa's ANC won barely over 30% in the 4 November local elections, its worst result, with final results due on 8 November.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20261107-%C3%A9lections-locales-en-afrique-du-sud-l-anc-signe-le-pire-r%C3%A9sultat-%C3%A9lectoral-de-son-histoire", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "world"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "South Africa's African National Congress (ANC) is heading for the worst result in its history in the local elections held on Wednesday 4 November, with barely more than 30% of the vote once over 95% of ballots had been counted, according to the electoral commission as reported by RFI. The ANC won 45% in the 2021 local elections and 40% in the 2024 general election. Turnout was about 50%, higher than at the last local vote, and RFI's correspondent says voters were punishing poor basic services and local corruption. The Democratic Alliance (DA), the main opposition party, keeps its absolute majority in Cape Town and could come first in Johannesburg, Pretoria and Port Elizabeth. Populist parties also gained: Jacob Zuma's MK party is on course to win most votes in Durban, and Herman Mashaba's ActionSA made gains around Johannesburg and Pretoria. Official final results are due on Sunday 8 November, and coalition talks have already started.", "why": "the party that has governed since 1994 already rules nationally in coalition, and it is now losing ground in the big cities too.", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-anc-slumps-to-its-worst-ever-result", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-04", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["South Africa", "ANC", "Democratic Alliance", "MK party"]}
{"id": "2026-11-07-news-eu-states-delay-methane-rules-by-a-year", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "EU states delay methane rules by a year", "summary": "EU member states approved on 6 November a one-year delay of the methane regulation to 2028, setting up talks with Parliament, which wants three years.", "url": "https://www.rfi.fr/fr/environnement/20261107-les-%C3%A9tats-europ%C3%A9ens-reportent-d-un-an-l-entr%C3%A9e-en-vigueur-de-la-l%C3%A9gislation-sur-le-m%C3%A9thane", "source_domain": "rfi.fr", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics", "economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "EU member states approved on Friday 6 November a one-year delay, from 2027 to 2028, to the EU methane regulation's obligations, three days before the COP31 climate summit opens on Monday 9 November. According to RFI, the Commission justified the delay by high energy prices caused by the Middle East war. France had asked for one year, the Czech Republic and Italy wanted longer, and Germany sided with France and the Commission. The European Parliament voted in October for a three-year delay, so the two institutions must now negotiate. The rules require oil and gas operators to find and repair methane leaks, and also apply to foreign producers whose fuel is imported into the EU, which the United States in particular has opposed. Methane is the second-largest driver of global warming after CO2.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-eu-states-delay-methane-rules-by-a-year", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["European Union", "EU methane regulation", "COP31"]}
{"id": "2026-11-07-news-us-sanctions-canadian-money-exchangers-over-iran", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "US sanctions Canadian money exchangers over Iran", "summary": "The US Treasury sanctioned eight Canadian money services businesses over Iran sanctions evasion and FinCEN cut off crypto exchange MEXC Global, coordinated with FINTRAC.", "url": "https://www.fincen.gov/news/news-releases/operation-economic-outcast-severs-mexc-globals-access-us-financial-system", "source_domain": "fincen.gov", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR", "new source"], "topics": ["economy", "geopolitics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "The US Treasury on Friday 6 November sanctioned eight Canadian money services businesses that it says help Iran evade sanctions, and its anti-money-laundering unit FinCEN cut the crypto exchange MEXC Global off from the US financial system. Canada's financial intelligence agency FINTRAC (the Financial Transactions and Reports Analysis Centre of Canada) said it worked with the US sanctions office OFAC and FinCEN on the measures. It added that since the start of 2026 it has revoked the registrations of 161 money services businesses, more than in the previous three years combined. FinCEN says MEXC Global, which is incorporated in the Seychelles, facilitated at least $16 billion in illicit transactions linked to sanctions evasion by Iran, North Korea and Russia, as well as cybercrime. Its order bars US financial institutions from processing transfers involving the exchange. Both actions fall under the Treasury's \"Operation Economic Outcast\" campaign against Iran's finances.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-us-sanctions-canadian-money-exchangers-over-iran", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["US Treasury", "FinCEN", "FINTRAC", "MEXC Global", "Iran"]}
{"id": "2026-11-07-news-vaud-seeks-chf-55-million-more-for-tram", "date": "2026-11-07", "stream": "news", "headline": "Vaud seeks CHF 55 million more for tram", "summary": "The Vaud Council of State asked the Grand Council for a CHF 55.3 million extra loan and a CHF 20 million higher guarantee to finish the Lausanne-Flon to Renens tram.", "url": "https://www.vd.ch/actualites/communiques-de-presse-de-letat-de-vaud/detail/communique/demande-de-credit-additionnel-pour-finaliser-la-premiere-etape-du-tramway-entre-lausanne-flon-et-la-gare-de-renens", "source_domain": "vd.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Vaud Council of State asked the cantonal parliament (Grand Council) on Thursday 5 November for an extra CHF 55.3 million loan and a CHF 20 million higher cantonal guarantee for the Lausanne public transport company (tl) to finish the first stage of the tram between Lausanne-Flon and Renens station. The money covers cost overruns since parliament approved the original credits in 2016. The canton says the main cause is objections and appeals against the planned Vigie-Gonin ramp, which delayed work by about eight years before the ramp was dropped for a road reorganisation. Other causes include redesigns such as the Galicien viaduct at Malley, changes around Renens station, measures against noise and vibration from more night services, inflation, and late delivery of trams after floods at the manufacturer's plant in Valencia, Spain. The Council of State and the Grand Council's finance committee had already allowed the extra spending in spring 2024, before a formal credit. Test runs are under way, and the line is due to open by the end of the year.", "why": "Lausanne's first tram is weeks from opening, but the bill has grown, and the Grand Council now has to approve the overruns afterwards.", "thread_id": "2026-11-07-news-vaud-seeks-chf-55-million-more-for-tram", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-07", "event_date": "2026-11-05", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Vaud", "Lausanne", "Renens", "tl"]}
